This module gathers and re-exports the neighbourhood-system development: domains given by neighbourhoods, through Examples 1.2 through 1.5 and Example B, Theorems 1.10 and 1.11, and Exercises 1.12 through 1.27; approximable mappings, through Examples 2.3 and 2.4 and Exercises 2.13 through 2.16, 2.18, and 2.20 through 2.22; the product, the function space, and Exercises 3.14 through 3.28, including the sum, iterated, and distributivity forms; fixed points and recursion, through Theorems 4.1 and 4.6, Examples 4.3 and 4.4, and Exercises 4.7 through 4.25; the typed lambda calculus, through Table 5.5, Theorems 5.1, 5.2, and 5.6, Propositions 5.3 and 5.4, and Exercises 5.7 through 5.16, including the Thue–Morse and overlap variants; domain equations, through Examples 6.1 and 6.2, Definitions 6.3, 6.8, 6.10, and 6.13, Propositions 6.6, 6.7, 6.11, and 6.12, Theorems 6.9, 6.14, and 6.16, Lemma 6.15, and Exercises 6.17 through 6.19 and 6.21 through 6.29; computability in effectively given domains, through the recursive notions, Definitions 7.1, 7.2, 7.9, and 7.11, Theorems 7.4 through 7.6, Propositions 7.7, 7.10, and 7.12, Example 7.8, the combinators of Proposition 7.7, and Exercises 7.13 through 7.24; and retracts of the universal domain, through Definitions 8.1, 8.3, 8.7, and 8.9, Propositions 8.2 and 8.10, Theorems 8.5, 8.6, and 8.8, Example 8.4, and Exercises 8.11 through 8.27, together with the supporting primitive-recursive, splitting, and presentation constructions.
